Z||000003||olympic||11-18-1999||05:31 PM||||I've seen people use the squarish vents from the hood of a Trans-Am. They mount them with the fins facing  backwards on their fiero so they let air out instead of ramming it in. I also thought that the vents from a Lumina Z34 might work. If you dont mind spending $500US you can get a custom made vented hood from F.O.C.O.A.||||reg||3
Z||000004||batboy||11-18-1999||08:37 PM||||The Pontaic dealer can order the 1986 vintage Trans Am louvers for less than $50.||||reg||1
Z||000005||CUTTER||11-18-1999||10:01 PM||||How would you put those Trans-Am louvers on then? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||11-18-1999||11:02 PM||||OMG! I misspelled Pontiac. Curses!<P>Cutter, I'm not 100% sure, but I've been told it's fairly straight forward task. Probably the hardest thing is gritting your teeth as you cut a couple holes into that nice hood. There are a couple old topics discussing the louver thing here on the forum. It's one of the mods I plan to eventually put on the Batmobile. I've heard they really help out with cooling and keeping the headlight covers from popping up at high speed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||11-18-1999||11:02 PM||||I've never done it but I assume that all you do is cut 2 holes in your fieros hood, using the vent as a template. Then mount the vents in the holes with glue or screws. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Greg Piet||11-18-1999||11:57 PM||||What about using the stock intake vents on the hood?  I dont have any spare ones to place and see how it may look, but just an idea since i figure there are a few extra ones out there from Holley scoops installed.||||reg||1
